    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is coherent, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase:\n\n- **`Owner.getDeleted()`/`setDeleted()`** sit alongside the other flag accessors (`getPossibleDuplicate()`, `getPossibleDuplicateOf()`) and mirror their style, including the Javadoc convention and the null-normalization already used elsewhere. The field initializer `= false` matches the spec's \"newly created owner is false.\"\n- **`deleteOwner()`** now does `setDeleted(true)` + `saveOwner(...)`, which directly parallels the existing `updateOwner`/`addPetToOwner` flow of mutate-then-save through `clinicService`. Logic stays at the same layer as the surrounding endpoints, so nothing new leaks into the service or model.\n- **`isNotDeleted(Owner)`** is a single private predicate placed next to the other small guard helper (`isBlank`), and it is reused via `.filter(this::isNotDeleted)` across all three checks (`rejectDuplicateIdentity`, the household-match, and the household in-use check) rather than duplicating the condition three times. Good factoring.\n- The schema changes are applied uniformly across all four DB dialects, and the OpenAPI `deleted` property is documented as `readOnly` consistent with the other derived fields in that schema.\n\nNo class or method is becoming a catch-all; the new logic lands in the units a maintainer would expect.\n\nOne cosmetic observation (not blocking): `isNotDeleted` uses `!Boolean.TRUE.equals(owner.getDeleted())` even though `getDeleted()` already collapses `null` to `false`, so `!owner.getDeleted()` would suffice. It's harmless and arguably defensive, so no change is required.",
